*	long int			|						|													|
*	long long int		|						|													|
*	----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*	std::nullptr_t		| Null pointer			| a null pointer									| nullptr
*	----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*	void				| void					| no type											| n/a
* 
*	We can notice that some of these type has the same category : integral. That's because in C++ "integral" means "like integer". So
*	in fact they are stored in memory as integer number, even if they have their own data type. For example "bool" is stored as 0 or 1
*	but in fact it is used in programmation to say true or false.
* 
*	In C++ there are already two built-in to the language itself sets of data types :
*		- Fundamental data types (the table just above)
*		- Compound data types (provided in lesson 12) : They provide more complex data types and allow for the creation of custom 
*			types
*	All the other data types need a header to be included.
* 
*	The last notice : the suffix _t means that this is a data type.
